[
  {
    "file": "src/main/java/org/jenkinsci/plugin/gitea/GiteaNotifier.java",
    "start": 114,
    "end": 116,
    "severity": "medium",
    "category": "error handling",
    "message": "The new version lookup makes publishing a NOT_BUILT result depend on a successful /version request. If that request fails—for example, with a transient 502 or 503—retryOnServerError propagates the exception before createCommitStatus is attempted. JobCompletedListener only logs the failure, so an existing pending status is never replaced even if the commit-status endpoint is available. Previously this result could be published as WARNING without querying the version. Catch version-discovery failures and fall back to WARNING when SKIPPED support cannot be established."
  }
]

[
  {
    "file": "src/main/java/org/jenkinsci/plugin/gitea/GiteaNotifier.java",
    "start": 114,
    "end": 116,
    "severity": "medium",
    "category": "error handling",
    "message": "The version lookup is now a prerequisite for publishing any NOT_BUILT status, but there is no fallback if that request fails. If /api/v1/version times out or returns an HTTP error even while the commit-status endpoint remains available, the exception escapes sendNotifications and JobCompletedListener only logs it (head 2e021e56de9104d8d124cac086cf6b208b6b4a88, lines 342–348). No terminal status is posted, potentially leaving the completed build marked pending indefinitely. Previously, NOT_BUILT published WARNING without this request (base 19c54c3e5d9cc19d47302688526d1b016dd2110d, lines 109–111). Fall back to WARNING when the capability lookup fails rather than dropping the notification."
  }
]

[
  {
    "file": "src/client.ts",
    "start": 1825,
    "end": 1829,
    "severity": "medium",
    "category": "credential leakage",
    "message": "The new API-key methods pass credential-bearing responses through requestV3, which logs the entire parsed response when debug is enabled. createApiKey therefore writes the generated data.key to stdout, and listApiKeys similarly logs all returned keys. This exposes usable account credentials to anyone with access to collected diagnostic logs. Redact key values before logging or suppress response-body logging for these endpoints. Evidence: src/client.ts at HEAD 550f7bc03fbc4e1760f9f4f4382cd612f7aeedf8, lines 186–190 (console logging), 293–301 (unredacted response logging), and 1807–1829 (the new API-key calls)."
  }
]

[
  {
    "file": "src/client.ts",
    "start": 1825,
    "end": 1829,
    "severity": "medium",
    "category": "credential disclosure",
    "message": "The new API-key endpoints send credential-bearing responses through requestV3(), which logs the entire response without redaction. When debug is enabled, createApiKey() writes the newly generated key to stdout, and listApiKeys() similarly exposes the returned keys. These credentials can consequently reach centralized logs and become accessible to log readers. Redact key values or suppress response-body logging for API-key endpoints. Evidence at HEAD 550f7bc03fbc4e1760f9f4f4382cd612f7aeedf8: src/client.ts:180-189 (console logging), 293-300 (unredacted response logging), and 1807-1829 (new endpoint calls)."
  }
]
